# =============================================================================
# NORA — format of `secrets.env` (what becomes `secrets.env.sops`)
# =============================================================================
#
# This is the TEMPLATE. The real file is called `secrets.env`, exists in clear only
# long enough to be edited, and is committed only in the encrypted `secrets.env.sops`
# form. Encryption rule and commands: see `.sops.yaml` next to it.
#
#   cp secrets.env.example secrets.env
#   $EDITOR secrets.env
#   sops --encrypt --input-type dotenv --output-type dotenv \
#        secrets.env > secrets.env.sops
#   shred -u secrets.env
#
# ## Why this file has FEWER variables than `.env.example`
#
# Deliberate split into two planes:
#
#   * `.env.example`  -> ALL the compose variables, including the boring
#     non-secret ones (domain, image tags, backup retention, toggles). It is the
#     cartography and the documentation.
#   * `secrets.env`   -> ONLY what cannot leak. It is the only file that needs
#     encryption, rotation and care about who reads it.
#
# Encrypting the image tags would backfire: every rollout (`API_TAG=sha-...`) would
# become an edit to an encrypted file, and the git diff would say nothing.
#
# Compose accepts a repeated `--env-file`, and the LAST one wins. The deploy
# assembles the final configuration like this:
#
#   sops --decrypt --input-type dotenv --output-type dotenv \
#        secrets.env.sops > /run/nora/secrets.env       # tmpfs, chmod 600
#   docker compose --env-file ./env.defaults --env-file /run/nora/secrets.env \
#                  up -d --wait
#
# where `env.defaults` is `.env.example` filled in only with the non-secrets.
# Prefer a single file? That works too: put it all in `secrets.env` and encrypt.
# Just know you have chosen to re-encrypt on every tag change.
#
# ## No value here may be the string "unset"
#
# Inherited from Bicep, which wrote `'unset'` into the Key Vault when a secret
# arrived empty. `NORA_INTEGRATIONS_ENC_KEY=unset` TAKES DOWN THE API BOOT (the
# TokenCipher runs Base64.decode in the bean constructor). For that one variable
# EMPTY is not safe either, and no longer starts: it used to mean OAuth tokens
# written to the database in the clear. Details in `.env.example` §top.
#
# ## Format
#
# Pure dotenv: `KEY=value`, one per line, no `export`, no quotes out of habit.
# A secret with `$` has to become `$$` (Compose interpolates the file). Generate
# everything in base64/hex and the problem does not appear.
# =============================================================================


# --- Edge -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Tunnel connector token. The stack's only inbound path.
CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# --- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# openssl rand -base64 32   (two DIFFERENT values — ADR 0022)
POSTGRES_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_PLATFORM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# RLS role passwords (ADR 0026/0028). openssl rand -hex 24
# Single source: they feed the ALTER ROLE in the database AND the API's .env.
NORA_APP_PASSWORD=
RLS_TELEMETRY_PASSWORD=
# Repeated in the format the API consumes (same value as RLS_TELEMETRY_PASSWORD).
NORA_TELEMETRY_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=
SPRING_FLYWAY_PASSWORD=

# --- RLS enforce cutover (stage 3) ------------------------------------------
# All six move together, or not at all. Leave them out entirely before the cutover.
# The full procedure, including what to do when the API refuses to start, is in
# docs/operations/rls-cutover-runbook.md.
#
# DATASOURCE_* switches the API's runtime connection to the NOBYPASSRLS role. Flyway must
# stay on the OWNER -- that is what makes nora_app subject to the policies rather than
# exempt from them -- so SPRING_FLYWAY_USER is in this list too. It defaults to
# POSTGRES_ADMIN_USER and setting it explicitly here is belt and braces.
#
# The telemetry trio is not optional under enforce: the API refuses to start without ALL
# THREE, because the alternative is an operator dashboard that silently reads zero.
#
# Placeholders go INSIDE the value, not in a trailing comment. Compose's dotenv parser
# strips a trailing comment, so `DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=  # same as NORA_APP_PASSWORD` becomes
# an EMPTY value, which then falls back to the admin password -- a login failure at boot
# with a misleading message.
#
# DATASOURCE_USERNAME=nora_app
# DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=<same value as NORA_APP_PASSWORD above>
# SPRING_FLYWAY_USER=nora_admin
# NORA_TELEMETRY_DATASOURCE_URL=jdbc:postgresql://postgres:5432/nora
# NORA_TELEMETRY_DATASOURCE_USERNAME=nora_telemetry
# NORA_TELEMETRY_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=<same value as RLS_TELEMETRY_PASSWORD above>
# NORA_RLS_ENFORCE=true

# --- Auth -------------------------------------------------------------------
# openssl rand -base64 48  (min. 32 chars; changing it invalidates all sessions)
JWT_SECRET=
# Only if JWT_ALGORITHM=RS256.
JWT_RS256_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=

# --- Worker (api -> worker, ADR 0023 §3-4) ----------------------------------
# openssl rand -hex 32. Sent as `X-Internal-Token` on /analyze, /split and /analyze-live.
# Required by the compose (`:?`) on BOTH the `api` and the `worker` services, with the same
# value. Distinct from NORA_PLATFORM_INTERNAL_TOKEN below, which goes the other way.
NORA_WORKER_INTERNAL_TOKEN=

# --- LLM providers ----------------------------------------------------------
OPENAI_API_KEY=
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# --- E-mail -----------------------------------------------------------------
RESEND_API_KEY=

# --- OAuth integrations (ADR 0031) ------------------------------------------
# AES-256-GCM: exactly 32 bytes in base64 -> openssl rand -base64 32
# REQUIRED: the compose declares it `:?`, so empty or unset stops the stack from coming
# up. An empty key meant OAuth access and refresh tokens stored as `plain:` in Postgres;
# that path is now local-dev only, behind NORA_INTEGRATIONS_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=true.
# "unset" = API does not boot (invalid base64). Never "unset".
NORA_INTEGRATIONS_ENC_KEY=
# openssl rand -hex 32
NORA_INTEGRATIONS_STATE_SECRET=

GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
SLACK_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
NOTION_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
TODOIST_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
LINEAR_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
MS_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
NORA_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TRELLO_API_KEY=

# The `*_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` are NOT here: a client id is public (it shows up in the
# authorize URL). They live in the non-secret plane, with the rest of the config.

# --- Control plane (ADR 0022/0023/0024) -------------------------------------
# openssl rand -hex 32, TWO distinct values (least privilege).
#
# NORA_PLATFORM_ADMIN_TOKEN is `${VAR:?}` in the compose, so leaving it blank HERE does not
# fall back to anything — the stack refuses to start. That is deliberate: blank in the code
# means the worker's service credential authenticates the operator console.
NORA_PLATFORM_INTERNAL_TOKEN=
NORA_PLATFORM_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# --- Observability ----------------------------------------------------------
# openssl rand -base64 24
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- Cloudflare Access ------------------------------------------------------
# CF_ACCESS_AUD and CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN are NOT secrets (public
# identifiers) and live in the non-secret plane. It was exactly this confusion
# that broke Azure: the AUD was registered as a GitHub Secret and read as
# `vars.` -> arrived empty -> apps/admin/src/lib/access.ts did fail-OPEN.
# access.ts fails CLOSED since 2026-08-16, so the same mistake would now take the
# console down instead of opening it — a better failure, still a failure.
# Do not repeat it by encrypting a public value and "losing" it in here.

# --- Registry (GHCR) --------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub PAT with ONLY `read:packages`. It is what deploy.sh's `ghcr_login()`
# uses to authenticate before the `compose pull`.
#
# TODAY IT CAN STAY EMPTY: the 4 packages under `ghcr.io/sf0rzin/nora-*` are PUBLIC
# (checked on 2026-08-08 with an anonymous `docker pull` from the VM). Without a token
# deploy.sh's `ghcr_login()` becomes a no-op and the pull works.
#
# Fill it in if some package goes private again -- the symptom is the deploy dying at
# the pull with `denied`/`manifest unknown`, not with an explicit authentication error.
#
# Minimal scope on purpose: `read:packages` gives no push, does not read private code
# and changes nothing. A leak here is far less serious than a `write:packages`.
GHCR_PULL_TOKEN=

# Login user. Empty = derived from IMAGE_PREFIX (`sf0rzin/nora` -> `sf0rzin`),
# which is the right thing in the normal case. Only fill it if the PAT is from another account.
GHCR_USER=

# --- Profile 'platform' -----------------------------------------------------
# `true` turns on the compose `platform` profile: `nora-admin` (operator console) and
# `nora-postgres-platform` (control plane database, separate from the product database).
#
# It lives here, and not in some other .env, because `deploy.sh` reads the whole config
# from this file (`envget`) — including what is not a secret. Without this, the profile
# would only come up with `--platform` on the command line, and the systemd timer, which
# runs `deploy.sh --if-changed` with no flags, would take the admin down on the first cycle.
#
# With the profile on, CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN and CF_ACCESS_AUD become genuinely
# mandatory: the compose uses `:?` on both, and since 2026-08-16 `access.ts` blocks
# every console route when either is empty rather than degrading to edge-only. Empty
# is an outage now instead of a silent downgrade, which is the trade this repo wanted.
NORA_PLATFORM_ENABLED=false
